We consider spherically symmetric mass distributions, where the mass density ρ(r)
depends only on the radial distance r from the center. The density represents the
mass per unit volume at radius r. The enclosed mass within radius r is defined as

M(r) = 4π ∫₀ʳ ρ(r′) r′² dr′

The following profiles are commonly used in stellar structure, galactic dynamics,
and cosmological halo modeling.

-----------------------------------------------------------------------

1. Einasto Profile (Einasto 1965)

Description:
A smoothly varying density profile characterized by a continuously changing
logarithmic slope. It provides an excellent fit to dark matter halos in numerical
simulations and avoids sharp cusps or breaks.

Expression:
ρ(r) = ρ₋₂ · exp{ - (2/α) [ (r / r₋₂)ᵅ − 1 ] }

Parameters:
ρ₋₂   : density at the radius r₋₂
r₋₂   : radius where d ln ρ / d ln r = −2
α     : shape parameter controlling curvature

-----------------------------------------------------------------------

2. Sérsic Profile (Sérsic 1963) — Surface Density (2D)

Description:
A phenomenological law describing the radial surface brightness or surface mass
density of galaxies. It applies to projected (two-dimensional) distributions and
does not directly describe volume density.

Expression:
I(R) = Iₑ · exp{ - bₙ [ (R / Rₑ)¹⁄ⁿ − 1 ] }

Parameters:
Iₑ   : surface density at the effective radius
Rₑ   : radius enclosing half the total luminosity
n    : Sérsic index (concentration parameter)
bₙ   : constant chosen so that Rₑ encloses half the total light

Note:
The Einasto profile is mathematically analogous to the Sérsic law, but applies to
three-dimensional volume densities rather than projected surface densities.

-----------------------------------------------------------------------

3. Navarro–Frenk–White (NFW) Profile (Navarro, Frenk & White 1996)

Description:
A cuspy density profile emerging from cold dark matter simulations. It features a
central r⁻¹ cusp and a steep r⁻³ falloff at large radii.

Expression:
ρ(r) = ρₛ / [ (r / rₛ) (1 + r / rₛ)² ]

Parameters:
ρₛ : characteristic density
rₛ : scale radius

-----------------------------------------------------------------------

4. Hernquist Profile (Hernquist 1990)

Description:
A finite-mass profile commonly used to model stellar bulges and elliptical galaxies.
It resembles the de Vaucouleurs profile in projection and has an analytic potential.

Expression:
ρ(r) = (M / 2π) · a / [ r (r + a)³ ]

Parameters:
M : total mass
a : scale length

-----------------------------------------------------------------------

5. Plummer Sphere (Plummer 1911)

Description:
A cored density profile with finite central density. Historically used for star
clusters and pedagogical models due to its analytic simplicity.

Expression:
ρ(r) = (3M / 4πa³) · (1 + r² / a²)⁻⁵ᐟ²

Parameters:
M : total mass
a : core radius

-----------------------------------------------------------------------

6. Singular Isothermal Sphere

Description:
An idealized model producing flat rotation curves. While useful analytically, it
has infinite mass and is not physically realistic at large radii.

Expression:
ρ(r) = σ² / (2π G r²)

Parameters:
σ : velocity dispersion
G : gravitational constant

-----------------------------------------------------------------------

All profiles above describe the radial concentration of mass per unit volume as a
function of distance from the center of a star, galaxy, or halo.
